WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.577
Think of ice cream on a hot day. More people want it, so shops can charge more and still sell more. Economists show this on a market diagram.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:31.406
Price goes up the side and quantity along the bottom. Demand, D, slopes down: people buy more when it is cheap. Supply, S, slopes up. Where they cross is the equilibrium, with price P e and quantity Q e.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:41.527
When something other than price changes, a whole curve moves. This is a shift. An increase moves the curve to the right. A decrease moves it to the left.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:58.486
Changes that affect buyers move demand: income, tastes, population, advertising, and the prices of substitutes and complements. Changes that affect producers move supply: costs, technology, weather, taxes and subsidies.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:07.756
Step one. Decide which curve moves, demand or supply, and whether it is an increase or a decrease.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:19.377
Step two. Draw the new curve parallel to the old one, on the correct side. Label it D one or S one. Here, supply has fallen, so S one is to the left.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:33.408
Step three. Find where the new curve crosses the curve that did not move. That is the new equilibrium. Draw a line across to the price axis, P one, and down to the quantity axis, Q one.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:49.902
Learn the four results. Demand right: price and quantity both rise. Demand left: both fall. Supply right: price falls, quantity rises. Supply left: price rises, quantity falls.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:02:05.718
Here is a real question from January 2020. Incomes rise. Show the effect on the market for wireless headphones, and label the new curve, price and quantity. Three marks.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:17.753
Income affects buyers, so demand moves. With more income, people can afford more headphones at every price. So demand increases, and shifts to the right.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:23.590
Draw D one parallel to D, to its right. Leave S where it is.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:39.412
D one crosses S at a higher point. Draw across to P one, above P e, and down to Q one, to the right of Q e. Price and quantity both rise. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:51.090
A common mistake is moving supply when the change affects buyers. Income is about buyers, so only demand moves.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:03:00.440
Another is marking P one and Q one at the old crossing, or forgetting the labels. Use the new crossing, and label all three.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:16.367
Now you try. A new machine cuts the cost of making bicycles. Show the effect on the market for bicycles, and label the new curve, price and quantity. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:30.872
Costs affect producers, so supply moves. Lower costs mean firms can supply more at every price. Supply increases, and shifts to the right.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:44.309
Draw S one to the right of S. It crosses D lower down. So P one is below P e, and Q one is to the right of Q e. Price falls and quantity rises.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:53.713
Remember: which curve and which way, draw and label it, then mark P one and Q one at the new crossing. Now try the practice questions.
